WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.967
Welcome to this Java tutorial on strings, where

00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:07.047
you'll use named operations called methods to

00:00:07.047 --> 00:00:10.059
clean and join text, measure its length, and

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:12.865
compare its contents while preserving the

00:00:12.865 --> 00:00:13.892
original entry.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.948
These operations help applications display names

00:00:17.948 --> 00:00:21.275
consistently, compare search terms, and process

00:00:21.275 --> 00:00:24.177
records, while keeping the original entry

00:00:24.177 --> 00:00:27.363
available to explain how a cleaned result was

00:00:27.363 --> 00:00:28.000
produced.

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:31.611
A campus workshop volunteer needs a greeting

00:00:31.611 --> 00:00:33.766
built from a participant's name.

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:37.000
The supplied name has two ordinary spaces before

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.347
it and two after it.

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:41.580
We'll keep that entry, prepare a version without

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.746
the outside spaces, and use the cleaned text in

00:00:44.746 --> 00:00:45.622
the greeting.

00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:48.724
The finished program should display the

00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:51.676
greeting, report the cleaned name's length, and

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:54.690
check its contents against an expected spelling.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.516
We'll first work through a different practice

00:00:57.516 --> 00:01:00.217
name so you can predict the final program's

00:01:00.217 --> 00:01:01.975
three output lines yourself.

00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:05.488
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and build

00:01:05.488 --> 00:01:07.106
that practice example.

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:15.036
We'll use the familiar starter structure.

00:01:15.036 --> 00:01:18.290
Main is the class containing our program, and

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.472
its main method is the named operation where

00:01:21.472 --> 00:01:22.701
execution begins.

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:25.954
The text-processing statements will go inside

00:01:25.954 --> 00:01:26.822
that method.

00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:35.479
Our practice participant is Leo.

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:38.836
String, with a capital S, is Java's ready-made

00:01:38.836 --> 00:01:39.931
class for text.

00:01:39.931 --> 00:01:43.361
We'll store the entered name with two spaces on

00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:46.718
each side so the program has an original value

00:01:46.718 --> 00:01:47.594
to preserve.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.907
Those spaces are inside the quotation marks, so

00:01:53.907 --> 00:01:55.981
they're part of the stored text.

00:01:55.981 --> 00:01:59.092
To prepare the name for display, we'll call trim

00:01:59.092 --> 00:02:02.139
on that text and store its returned result in a

00:02:02.139 --> 00:02:04.408
separate variable called cleanName.

00:02:04.408 --> 00:02:07.519
For these ordinary spaces, trim removes only the

00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:09.010
ones at the boundaries.

00:02:12.766 --> 00:02:15.833
Trim is a method, a named operation supplied by

00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:16.290
String.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.226
The empty parentheses mean this call needs no

00:02:19.226 --> 00:02:20.009
extra input.

00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:22.489
Its returned text goes into cleanName.

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:25.230
Strings are immutable: their existing text

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.166
doesn't change in place, so enteredName still

00:02:28.166 --> 00:02:29.276
keeps the spaces.

00:02:29.276 --> 00:02:32.408
We'll make that difference visible when we print

00:02:32.408 --> 00:02:33.191
the results.

00:02:33.204 --> 00:02:36.358
With a cleaned name available, we can build the

00:02:36.358 --> 00:02:36.962
greeting.

00:02:36.962 --> 00:02:39.445
Joining text is called concatenation.

00:02:39.445 --> 00:02:42.264
Here the plus operators combine a greeting

00:02:42.264 --> 00:02:45.150
label, the cleaned name, and an exclamation

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:45.485
mark.

00:02:45.485 --> 00:02:48.572
The space after the comma belongs to the first

00:02:48.572 --> 00:02:50.854
piece; Java doesn't add it for us.

00:02:55.544 --> 00:02:58.215
Greeting now holds the combined text, while

00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:00.638
cleanName still holds the cleaned name.

00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:03.557
Let's print the original entry between brackets

00:03:03.557 --> 00:03:06.414
so its boundary spaces are visible, then print

00:03:06.414 --> 00:03:08.278
the greeting on the next line.

00:03:08.278 --> 00:03:11.073
The brackets are display markers we add here,

00:03:11.073 --> 00:03:13.744
not characters stored in the original name.

00:03:20.997 --> 00:03:23.317
These two lines will let us compare the

00:03:23.317 --> 00:03:25.518
preserved input with the new message.

00:03:25.518 --> 00:03:28.077
We also want a measure of the cleaned name.

00:03:28.077 --> 00:03:30.694
Calling length returns a whole-number count.

00:03:30.694 --> 00:03:33.371
For the ordinary English letters in Leo, that

00:03:33.371 --> 00:03:34.264
count is three.

00:03:34.264 --> 00:03:36.882
More complex text can use more than one Java

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:39.380
text position for a displayed symbol; this

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.522
example stays with ordinary letters.

00:03:45.412 --> 00:03:48.204
The length call inspects cleanName without

00:03:48.204 --> 00:03:49.001
changing it.

00:03:49.001 --> 00:03:51.726
To inspect the actual spelling, we'll use

00:03:51.726 --> 00:03:52.192
equals.

00:03:52.192 --> 00:03:55.050
This method takes an argument, the value we

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.841
supply for comparison, and returns true or

00:03:57.841 --> 00:03:58.240
false.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.098
Our practice comparison uses uppercase LEO,

00:04:01.098 --> 00:04:04.023
which differs from the capitalization of the

00:04:04.023 --> 00:04:04.887
cleaned name.

00:04:09.173 --> 00:04:12.050
Equals compares the text exactly, including

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.262
letter case, so this practice comparison will be

00:04:15.262 --> 00:04:15.664
false.

00:04:15.664 --> 00:04:18.809
We'll close the starter structure, then use the

00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:21.954
completed program to check the preserved entry,

00:04:21.954 --> 00:04:24.898
the greeting, the count, and the comparison.

00:04:26.841 --> 00:04:29.173
The practice program is complete.

00:04:29.173 --> 00:04:32.354
Let's click Run and see how the returned text

00:04:32.354 --> 00:04:34.616
differs from the original entry.

00:04:40.004 --> 00:04:42.899
Inside the brackets, Leo still has its original

00:04:42.899 --> 00:04:43.330
spaces.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:46.286
The greeting uses the cleaned name, and the next

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:49.119
line reports three, the cleaned name's length.

00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:52.076
False tells us that the cleaned spelling doesn't

00:04:52.076 --> 00:04:53.800
exactly match uppercase LEO.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.572
Together, these results show that the methods

00:04:56.572 --> 00:04:59.467
returned useful information without editing the

00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:00.329
original text.

00:05:00.402 --> 00:05:03.424
Now we can apply that pattern to the workshop's

00:05:03.424 --> 00:05:05.032
actual participant, Maya.

00:05:05.032 --> 00:05:07.796
We'll replace the practice statements while

00:05:07.796 --> 00:05:09.983
keeping the class and main method.

00:05:09.983 --> 00:05:13.005
The new entry again includes two spaces on each

00:05:13.005 --> 00:05:15.898
side, and we'll keep the original and cleaned

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:16.927
values separate.

00:05:23.602 --> 00:05:26.744
The new entry and the call that prepares it are

00:05:26.744 --> 00:05:27.345
in place.

00:05:27.345 --> 00:05:30.487
We'll build the greeting from the returned text

00:05:30.487 --> 00:05:32.626
and display that greeting first.

00:05:32.626 --> 00:05:35.835
The extra bracketed diagnostic from the practice

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:38.709
run isn't part of the workshop's three-line

00:05:38.709 --> 00:05:39.177
result.

00:05:49.411 --> 00:05:51.598
The greeting statement is ready.

00:05:51.598 --> 00:05:54.809
We'll finish with the same length operation and

00:05:54.809 --> 00:05:58.089
a content comparison using the spelling shown in

00:05:58.089 --> 00:05:58.773
this call.

00:05:58.773 --> 00:06:01.711
Neither check changes the text it inspects.

00:06:10.596 --> 00:06:13.480
Before we run this version, write its three

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.955
output lines in order.

00:06:14.955 --> 00:06:18.040
Use the practice example to trace the returned

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.722
trimmed text, the pieces joined into the

00:06:20.722 --> 00:06:23.874
greeting, the length, and the exact comparison.

00:06:23.874 --> 00:06:26.221
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.461
Let's click Run to compare your prediction with

00:06:37.461 --> 00:06:39.950
the workshop program's actual result.

00:06:44.533 --> 00:06:46.221
The greeting is Hello, Maya!

00:06:46.221 --> 00:06:49.115
It has the space supplied after the comma and no

00:06:49.115 --> 00:06:50.863
extra spaces around the name.

00:06:50.863 --> 00:06:52.491
Four is the cleaned length.

00:06:52.491 --> 00:06:55.384
True means the cleaned text exactly matches Maya

00:06:55.384 --> 00:06:57.133
with the same capitalization.

00:06:57.133 --> 00:07:00.026
Check those three lines against your prediction.

00:07:00.026 --> 00:07:02.860
The original enteredName still holds the padded

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.633
entry, just as it did in our practice example.

00:07:05.737 --> 00:07:08.446
We've built the volunteer's greeting and two

00:07:08.446 --> 00:07:10.108
checks of its cleaned name.

00:07:10.108 --> 00:07:13.063
Trim returned text we could store, concatenation

00:07:13.063 --> 00:07:15.649
built a new message, and length and equals

00:07:15.649 --> 00:07:17.927
reported information about that text.

00:07:17.927 --> 00:07:20.821
Preserving the entry separately lets us explain

00:07:20.821 --> 00:07:23.160
how the displayed result was produced.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.054
An exact spelling comparison checks text, not a

00:07:26.054 --> 00:07:27.162
person's identity.

00:07:27.874 --> 00:07:30.658
For your next experiment, change only the

00:07:30.658 --> 00:07:33.579
entered name to lowercase maya, keeping the

00:07:33.579 --> 00:07:34.869
surrounding spaces.

00:07:34.869 --> 00:07:37.314
Leave the comparison text unchanged.

00:07:37.314 --> 00:07:40.234
Which output lines should change, and which

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:41.660
should stay the same?

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.581
Explain your prediction before you run that

00:07:44.581 --> 00:07:45.124
version.
